  "scenario_name": "throttle-window-anchors-on-the-first-event",
  "scenario_description": "Source: upstream `part-time-simple-test` in riemann/test/riemann/streams_test.clj, which tests the window mechanism `throttle` is built on. Adjusted, because this is the mechanism riemann-go deliberately departed from, so the test cannot be ported as a claim about what riemann-go does. What it can do is name the observable difference and assert riemann-go's side of it.\nUpstream's test takes an anchor at construction time and divides wall time into fixed bins from it. With an anchor at 1 and a window of 5 the bins are [1,6), [6,11), [11,16) and so on forever, whether or not any event arrives. The test's own output shows what that means across a gap: events arrive at 1, 2 and 4, then at 6, 8 and 9, then nothing until 99. The window that receives the t=99 event is [96,101), a bin whose start precedes the event by three units, and t=101 opens the next bin rather than continuing the one t=99 started. Nothing about the gap resets the grid.\nSEMANTICS.md `throttle` states riemann-go's rule instead: \"A window opens when an event arrives for a fork key with no open window, and it closes `window_seconds` of event time later\", and classifies the change as a heuristic replacing an accident, on the grounds that no upstream test depends on the anchor's value and that a grid derived from process start time cannot be reproduced under a scenario timeline. This scenario is the case that makes the two rules disagree, which is the gap, and it is the assertion that no upstream test carries.\nThe timeline is a burst, a gap longer than a window, and a second burst straddling where the fixed grid's boundary would fall. Under first-event anchoring the second burst's window opens at metric 4 and closes 2 s later, so metric 6 is the third event in one window and is discarded. Under a grid anchored anywhere near the rule's installation, metric 6 falls in a fresh bin and passes. That one event is the whole difference and the scenario names it.\nThe verdict does not depend on SEMANTICS.md Open question 2. Under the sliding reading of SPEC.md property 9, metric 6 at t=4.1 is preceded by two passed events within the previous 2 s and is discarded as well.\nRescaled from upstream's units: a window of 5 becomes 2 s and upstream's 90-unit silence becomes a 1.8 s gap, which is longer than one window and is all the property needs. Upstream advances a test clock explicitly; here the offsets are real wall clock, so the second burst is placed 0.9 s clear of the first window's close rather than on top of it.\nWrong implementations this catches: a throttle whose windows are fixed divisions of wall time from an anchor taken at startup or at rule installation, which admits metric 6; a throttle whose window never closes, which discards metrics 4, 5 and 6; a throttle that resets its count on every event rather than on the window boundary, which admits everything; an off-by-one admitting a third event per window, which admits metric 3.\nExpected wall clock: about 4.1 s of stimulus plus a 4 s settle.\n",
